{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Learning Experience Design and Software Technology","title":"Comparative Software Audit","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting comparative software audit to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-01","statement":"Build a comparative audit matrix that scores every candidate tool on the same task-derived criteria"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish a pass-or-fail gate from a weighted dimension and apply gates before reading totals"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-03","statement":"State a recommendation bounded by its named context (learners, budget, constraints) plus the re-entry condition that would change the winner"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-04","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with an accepted and a rejected suggestion, such as using BigAL to attack the criteria rather than rank the tools"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology","ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/02/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Comparative Software Audit\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Comparative Software Audit, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/02-07-comparative-software-audit.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Comparative Software Audit: Comparative Software Audit, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nChoosing a learning tool is one of the highest-leverage decisions a designer makes, because it sets the affordances and constraints every later design must live inside. Yet the most common way to choose is the least defensible: praise each candidate on whatever it happens to do best (\"this one is cheap, that one has the most templates, the other is newest\") and pick the one with the nicest-sounding strength. This section teaches the disciplined alternative, a comparative audit that scores every candidate on the same criteria so the comparison is actually a comparison.\n\nThe two structural ideas are shared criteria and gates. **Shared criteria** mean every tool is judged on the same dimensions, so a verdict reflects fit to your task rather than which feature each vendor chose to highlight. A **gate** is a non-negotiable requirement (often accessibility, security, or budget) that a tool must satisfy to remain eligible at all; a gate is pass-or-fail, not a weighted score that a strength elsewhere can offset. Confusing a gate with a weight is the error that lets a tool with a fatal flaw win on points.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Build a comparative audit matrix that scores every candidate tool on the same task-derived criteria.\n- Distinguish a pass-or-fail gate from a weighted dimension and apply gates before reading totals.\n- State a recommendation bounded by its named context (learners, budget, constraints) plus the re-entry condition that would change the winner.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with an accepted and a rejected suggestion, such as using BigAL to attack the criteria rather than rank the tools.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team must pick an authoring tool for a cohort that includes screen-reader users, on a fixed budget. The first draft of the audit reads: \"Tool A is cheap, Tool B has the most templates, Tool C is the newest, so let us go with Tool B for its template library.\" It sounds reasonable and it is indefensible, because no two tools were judged on the same dimension. The recommendation rests on a feature count, and the feature that would actually disqualify Tool B, its lack of screen-reader support, was never even in the comparison.\n\nThe case exposes why an unsound comparison can feel complete. Each tool was described accurately; the failure was structural, not factual. Without shared criteria there is no common ground to stand a verdict on, and without a gate the accessibility requirement that should have settled the decision was invisible. The audit must record the criteria and the gate so a reviewer can see that the comparison was fair before trusting its conclusion.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA defensible comparative audit is built in a fixed order, and the order is what makes it fair. First, define the task precisely, including the learners and the constraints (\"let novice designers build a branching scenario with screen-reader support, on a fixed budget\"), because the task determines which criteria matter. Second, derive the criteria from the task, not from the tools, so the dimensions exist before any candidate is scored. Third, separate criteria into **weighted dimensions**, where more is better and a strength can offset a weakness, and **gates**, where failure is disqualifying regardless of other scores. Fourth, score every tool on every criterion using the same scale. Fifth, apply the gates before reading the totals, so a tool that fails a gate is removed even if it leads on points.\n\nThe decisive concept is the gate-versus-weight distinction. Accessibility for a cohort with screen-reader users is a gate: a tool that cannot serve those learners cannot be chosen, and no number of templates can buy back that failure. Treating accessibility as a mere weight (say, one criterion among five) is the technical mistake that lets a high template score rescue an inaccessible tool. Gates encode requirements that are categorical, not tradeable.\n\nThe course anchors decide which criteria deserve gate status:\n\n- **UDL learner variability** is usually what elevates accessibility from a weight to a gate, because a tool that excludes a class of learners has failed the task, not merely scored low.\n- **Cognitive load theory** informs the \"novice learnability\" criterion, since a tool that demands heavy interface learning taxes designers before they reach the design work.\n- **Mayer's principles** inform criteria about how the tool lets you build (signaling, contiguity, control), connecting the audit back to what the tool must afford for good multimedia design.\n\nA defensible comparative audit is built in a fixed order, and the order is what makes it fair. Put the five steps in sequence.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Define the task precisely, including the learners and constraints (novice designers, branching scenario, screen-reader support, fixed budget)\",\"Derive the criteria from the task, not from the tools, so dimensions exist before any candidate is scored\",\"Separate the criteria into weighted dimensions and pass-or-fail gates\",\"Score every tool on every criterion using the same scale\",\"Apply the gates before reading the totals, removing any tool that fails a gate\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"In the audit, Tool B has the highest template count and a strong total but scores 0 on screen-reader support, which is marked as a gate for a cohort that includes screen-reader users. Can its template lead rescue it?\" explanation=\"A gate is categorical, not tradeable. Accessibility for screen-reader users is a pass-or-fail requirement, so a 0 there removes Tool B before totals are read. Treating accessibility as one weight among five is the technical mistake that lets a strength elsewhere buy back a fatal flaw.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, because the highest total should win\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, a failed gate disqualifies the tool before totals matter, regardless of any other strength\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, if the template advantage is large enough to offset the gap\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Building a Comparative Audit Matrix\n\nHere is a before-and-after of an audit comparing three authoring tools for a task defined as \"let novice designers build a branching scenario with screen-reader support, on a fixed budget.\"\n\n**Before (unsound comparison)**\n\nThe draft praised each tool on a different axis: \"Tool A is cheap, Tool B has the most templates, Tool C is the newest.\" It then picked Tool B for its template count. No two tools were judged on the same dimension.\n\n**The decision point.** The audit was rebuilt around five shared criteria, scored 0 to 2 for every tool, with accessibility marked as a non-negotiable gate.\n\n| Criterion (gate marked *) | Tool A | Tool B | Tool C |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Branching authoring | 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| Screen-reader support * | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| Novice learnability | 2 | 1 | 1 |\n| Cost within budget | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n| Export to LMS | 1 | 2 | 2 |\n| Total | 9 | 7 | 6 |\n\n**After (defensible recommendation)**\n\nTool B had the most templates but scored 0 on the accessibility gate, which disqualifies it for a cohort that includes screen-reader users. Tool A wins on both total score and the gate.\n\nAudit note (decision level): \"I scored all three tools on one shared criteria set rather than praising each on its own strength. Accessibility is a gate, not a weight, so Tool B's template lead cannot rescue a 0 there. The recommendation holds for novice designers on this budget with screen-reader users; if that context changed, Tool B could re-enter.\"\n\nThe audit is defensible because the verdict rests on common criteria, an explicit gate, and a named context, not on a feature count or a per-tool compliment.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDerive the criteria from the task before you look at any tool, and apply gates before you read totals. A comparison is only fair if every candidate met the same bar, and only valid if a fatal flaw cannot be outscored.\n\nBefore you trust an audit's recommendation, check it against these questions:\n\n1. **Shared criteria:** Was every tool scored on the same dimensions, or was each praised on its own strength?\n2. **Task-derived:** Do the criteria come from the task and its learners, or from the tools' marketing?\n3. **Gate handling:** Which criteria are gates (pass/fail), and were they applied before totals so no strength could buy back a gate failure?\n4. **Named context:** Does the recommendation state the learners, budget, and constraints under which it holds, so a reader knows when it would change?\n\nAn audit that passes is a defensible recommendation. One that compares feature counts across mismatched axes is a preference with a table around it.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading treats the highest total as the winner without checking the gates. In the worked example Tool B scores well on templates but zero on the accessibility gate, so its total is irrelevant; a tool that fails a gate is out before totals are read. A scoring table without enforced gates can recommend a tool that cannot legally or ethically be used.\n\nThe second misreading collapses \"newest\" or \"most features\" into \"best.\" Recency and feature count are properties of the tool, not measures of fit to your task. A two-year-old tool that affords screen-reader-friendly branching beats a brand-new one that does not, for a cohort that needs branching with screen readers. Always score fit to the named task, and let the criteria, not the vendor's framing, decide what counts.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRead the worked example's matrix as a machine for producing a defensible verdict. Every tool is scored 0 to 2 on the same five criteria, so the columns are comparable; this alone fixes the original draft's fatal flaw. Then notice the asterisk on \"screen-reader support,\" which marks it as a gate. Tool B posts a strong total on templates and export, but its 0 on the gated criterion removes it from contention before the totals matter. The matrix lets you see, at a glance, that template strength cannot rescue an accessibility failure, because the gate is enforced separately from the sum.\n\nWatch how the recommendation is then stated with its boundary conditions. Tool A wins \"for novice designers on this budget with screen-reader users,\" and the audit explicitly notes that \"if that context changed, Tool B could re-enter.\" That clause is not hedging; it is the mark of a rigorous audit, because it tells a future reader exactly which assumption would overturn the verdict. A recommendation without its conditions cannot be reused safely.\n\nWork the gate-versus-weight decision as a tree. A stakeholder pushes back on your audit.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Gate versus weight under pressure\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A manager says: Tool B has far more templates and scores well overall, so let us pick it; treat accessibility as just one more criterion. How do you respond?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Agree and average accessibility in as a fifth weighted criterion\",\"next\":\"weight\",\"feedback\":\"This lets a template lead buy back a 0 on accessibility, recommending a tool a class of learners cannot use.\"},{\"label\":\"Explain that accessibility is a gate for this cohort: pass-or-fail, applied before totals\",\"next\":\"gate\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: a categorical requirement is not tradeable against template count.\"},{\"label\":\"Pick the highest total and note the accessibility concern in a footnote\",\"next\":\"footnote\",\"feedback\":\"A footnote does not enforce a gate; the inaccessible tool still gets recommended.\"}]},\"gate\":{\"prompt\":\"The manager asks: is there ANY condition under which Tool B could win?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, name the re-entry condition: if the cohort no longer includes screen-reader users, accessibility relaxes to a weight and Tool B competes\",\"next\":\"reentry\",\"feedback\":\"Naming the re-entry condition shows the verdict is reasoned, not a fixed preference.\"},{\"label\":\"No, Tool B can never be chosen\",\"next\":\"reentry\",\"feedback\":\"Overstated: the verdict is a function of the task and gates, so a changed task can change the winner.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"reentry\":{\"prompt\":\"Resolved: Tool A wins for this cohort and budget, with a stated re-entry condition for Tool B if the context changes.\",\"feedback\":\"Gates enforce categorical requirements; the named context makes the verdict portable and falsifiable.\",\"choices\":[]},\"weight\":{\"prompt\":\"Tool B is selected, and at launch screen-reader users cannot complete the branching step.\",\"feedback\":\"Collapsing a gate into a weight is exactly how a tool with a fatal flaw wins on points.\",\"choices\":[]},\"footnote\":{\"prompt\":\"The footnote is overlooked in approval, and the inaccessible tool ships.\",\"feedback\":\"A gate that is not enforced before totals is not a gate.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe walkthrough's lesson is that the structure carries the defensibility. Shared criteria make the comparison fair, the gate makes a categorical requirement categorical, and the named context makes the verdict portable to anyone who shares those conditions and falsifiable for anyone who does not.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, look at any \"best tools\" comparison you can find online and test it for shared criteria and gates. Most fail: they praise each product on a different strength and never declare a non-negotiable requirement. Spotting that failure trains the judgment to build audits that do not commit it, and it makes you a harder person to sell a tool to.\n\nA strong reader of comparative audits can answer four questions about any tool recommendation:\n\n1. Were all candidates scored on the same task-derived criteria?\n2. Which requirements are gates, and were they enforced before totals?\n3. Under exactly which learners, budget, and constraints does the verdict hold?\n4. What single change in context would make a different tool win?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong artifact for this section is an audit matrix with task-derived criteria, at least one explicit gate, identical scoring across all candidates, and a recommendation that names its context. The strongest versions add the re-entry condition: the specific change that would let a currently disqualified tool win, which proves the verdict is reasoned rather than a fixed preference.\n\nWhen you use BigAL on an audit, ask it to attack your criteria, not to rank the tools. A useful prompt is: \"Here is my task and my five criteria, one marked as a gate. Which criterion am I missing for this cohort, and is anything I weighted actually a gate?\" Getting the criteria and gates right is where audits succeed or fail, so that is where the tool's scrutiny is most valuable.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same three tools and change the task: the cohort no longer includes screen-reader users, but the deliverable must integrate with a strict student-data-privacy regime. The audit's machinery is unchanged, but two things move. The accessibility gate relaxes from a gate to a weight, which is exactly the \"re-entry condition\" the original audit named, so Tool B's template lead now counts. Meanwhile a new gate appears, data-privacy compliance, and whichever tool fails it is removed regardless of its template count. The same comparison, re-run with a different gate, can crown a different winner. If you can predict that Tool B re-enters when accessibility stops being a gate, but is removed again if it fails the privacy gate, you understand that an audit's verdict is a function of its task and gates, not a fixed ranking of tools.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/02-07-comparative-software-audit-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"02.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 02.07. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Comparative Software Audit, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit","stem":"A software audit recommends Tool A because \"it has 30 features versus Tool B's 12.\" What is wrong with this conclusion?","options":["Thirty features is too many for any learning tool.","It counts features rather than fit; the audit never asks which features actually serve the defined learning task.","Tool B should win because simpler is always better.","Feature counts are fine as long as both numbers are verified."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A comparative audit judges fit to the task. A long feature list can include capabilities irrelevant to the learning goal.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_more_features_wins","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit","stem":"An audit praises Tool A for low price and Tool B for strong accessibility, then picks Tool A. Why is the comparison unsound?","options":["Price should never be an audit criterion.","The tools were scored on different axes, so no head-to-head judgment was made; each must be scored on the same shared criteria set.","Tool B should automatically win on accessibility alone.","Two criteria are too few for any audit."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A real comparison requires one common criteria set applied to every tool. Praising each on a different dimension prevents an actual head-to-head verdict.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_no_shared_criteria","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit","stem":"An audit concludes \"Tool C is the best authoring tool.\" What does a defensible audit add to that claim?","options":["A trophy graphic to emphasize the winner.","The context the recommendation assumes: the learners, setting, budget, and constraints under which Tool C wins, so a reader knows when the verdict would change.","A statement that Tool C is best for everyone everywhere.","The release date of Tool C."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"There is no context-free best tool. Naming the assumed learners, setting, and constraints makes the recommendation falsifiable and transferable.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_context_free_winner","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_comparative_software_audit"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your audit scored three tools on the same five criteria and Tool B won overall but failed the accessibility criterion outright. What is the strongest evidence-based revision to the recommendation?","options":["Keep Tool B as the flat winner because it had the best total score.","Flag accessibility as a non-negotiable gate: if your learners include screen-reader users, Tool B is disqualified despite its total, and the recommendation shifts to the next tool that passes the gate.","Drop the accessibility criterion so Tool B can win cleanly.","Average the scores again to smooth out the accessibility failure."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Some criteria are gates, not weights. A defensible audit lets a non-negotiable failure override a high total when the context requires it.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_gate_treated_as_weight","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-02-07-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_more_features_wins","pattern":"feature_count_as_quality","description":"Judging tools by how many features they list rather than how well each tool serves the specific learning task","trigger":"learner recommends the tool with the longest feature list without tying features to the task","feedback":"Count fit, not features. A tool wins the audit when it best serves the defined task, even if it has fewer features than a rival.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_no_shared_criteria","pattern":"comparison_without_common_criteria","description":"Comparing tools on different dimensions each so no head-to-head judgment is actually possible","trigger":"learner praises Tool A for price and Tool B for accessibility without scoring both on the same criteria","feedback":"Define one shared criteria set and score every tool against all of it. Praising each tool on a different axis is not a comparison.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_context_free_winner","pattern":"best_tool_independent_of_context","description":"Naming a single best tool as if it were best for every context, ignoring learners, budget, and setting","trigger":"learner declares a universal winner without naming the context the recommendation holds for","feedback":"There is no context-free best tool. State the learners, setting, and constraints your recommendation assumes, so a reader can tell when it would change.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_7_comparative_software_audit_gate_treated_as_weight","pattern":"gate_compensated_by_other_scores","description":"Averaging a non-negotiable requirement such as accessibility into the weighted total so a strength elsewhere can buy back a fatal failure","trigger":"learner lets a high weighted total or a large feature lead override a zero on a gated criterion","feedback":"A gate is pass-or-fail and is applied before any totals are read. No strength on another dimension can offset a gate failure; a tool that fails a gate is removed from contention, however high its weighted score.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"762f40c5e3659feb4701a308116ece842a3c513f4e0a6b582f9a63c359b1d7b6","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}